Ddoc

$(SPEC_S Introduction,

$(HEADERNAV_TOC)

$(P Laser-D, pronounced "laser D", is Lesser-D: a deliberately smaller dialect
of the D programming language. It retains D syntax and selected D frontend
machinery while defining a more explicit language with a C-like runtime model.)

$(P Laser-D is not defined as "D with BetterC enabled". BetterC is an
implementation mode rather than a complete language specification. Laser-D is
defined by this reference, the compiler implementation, and the focused
positive and negative tests under $(D compiler/test/laser-d).)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 goals, Language Goals))

$(UL
$(LI Every Laser-D program is also a D program; Laser-D introduces no new
syntax.)
$(LI Programs compile to native code without a garbage collector or D runtime
contract beyond supported C interoperability.)
$(LI Storage, control flow, cleanup, ordinary function calls, and external
interaction should be visible in source wherever practical.)
$(LI Compile-time facilities may provide zero-runtime abstraction but cannot
restore a rejected runtime or source-language feature.)
$(LI The language remains portable across Windows, Linux, and macOS, initially
on x86-64.)
$(LI A feature is supported only when it has a clear and robust contract across
the supported language surface. A useful special case is not sufficient.)
)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 conformance, Conformance))

$(P A source program conforms to Laser-D when it uses only constructs classified
as supported by this specification and is accepted by a conforming Laser-D
implementation. Accepted constructs remain subject to every cross-cutting
restriction stated elsewhere in this reference.)

$(P The tests under $(D compiler/test/laser-d) are the executable language
specification. Compilable and runnable tests demonstrate accepted behavior;
fail-compilation tests demonstrate syntax and semantics that a conforming
implementation must reject. When implementation, prose, and tests disagree,
the disagreement is a specification defect that must be resolved rather than
treated as an undocumented extension.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 document-status, Document Status))

$(P This reference is being converted from the upstream D language
specification. Chapters and productions are classified as follows:)

$(UL
$(LI $(B Normative): defines supported or deliberately restricted Laser-D
behavior.)
$(LI $(B Excluded): describes a D facility that is not part of Laser-D. An
exclusion statement is normative; retained upstream explanatory text is not.)
$(LI $(B Under review): records an open area and does not grant a portability or
compatibility guarantee.)
)

$(P Upstream D text that has not yet been rewritten is background material only.
It does not override a Laser-D decision, add a grammar production, or make an
unclassified D feature part of Laser-D.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 runtime-model, Runtime Model))

$(P Laser-D uses explicit, caller-controlled storage. Fixed-size arrays and
structs contain their storage directly. Dynamic-array values are non-owning
pointer-and-length views. Pointers and C allocation APIs remain available, but
the language does not provide GC allocation, associative arrays, classes,
exceptions, runtime type metadata, or module lifecycle machinery.)

$(P All functions are implicitly $(D nothrow), $(D @nogc), and $(D @system). Laser-D is
not a memory-safe language: pointer validity, ownership, aliasing, and the
lifetime of non-owning views remain programmer responsibilities.)

$(P Constructors and modern operator overloads are narrow exceptions to the
preference for explicit calls. They are retained for fixed-storage,
primitive-like value abstractions such as matrices, big integers, and
fixed-precision decimals, and remain subject to every other Laser-D
restriction.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 compilation, Compilation Model))

$(P Compilation follows the DMD frontend phases: source decoding, lexical
analysis, parsing, semantic analysis, optional optimization, and native code
generation. Laser-D restrictions are diagnosed by the earliest reliable phase.
The frontend/backend interface and backend are inherited from DMD.)

$(H2 $(LNAME2 arithmetic, Arithmetic))

$(P Supported integer arithmetic uses the target's two's-complement
representation. Ordinary integer overflow is not checked unless a later
Laser-D decision states otherwise.)

$(P $(D float) and $(D double) use IEEE-754 arithmetic. Extended, imaginary, complex,
and vector source types are not part of Laser-D.)

$(SPEC_SUBNAV_NEXT lex, Lexical)
)

Macros:
TITLE = Laser-D Introduction
CHAPTER = 1
